Q: How to call a statically linked Pascal function from a C main? A: <p><code>writeln</code> needs the Free Pascal RTL to be initialized, but your C <code>main</code> skips FPC's own startup. The segfault in <code>SYSTEM_$$_FINALIZEUNITS</code> happens because the finalizer is trying to clean up units that were never set up in the first place.</p>
<p>I'd fix this by manually calling the init/exit pair and linking the startup glue object <code>si_c.o</code> from your RTL directory:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>/* main.c */
extern void fpc_initializeunits(void);
extern void fpc_do_exit(void);
void hello(void);

int main(void) {
    fpc_initializeunits();
    hello();
    fpc_do_exit();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>fpc hello.pas
gcc main.c hello.o \
    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/fpc/3.2.2/units/x86_64-linux/rtl/si_c.o \
    /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/fpc/3.2.2/units/x86_64-linux/rtl/system.o
</code></pre>
<p>Linking <code>system.o</code> alone isn't enough to handle the I/O, heap, and unit tables. So you need <code>si_c.o</code> to act as the bridge between glibc and the FPC runtime. Calling <code>fpc_initializeunits</code> ensures every unit's <code>initialization</code> block actually runs.</p>
